import OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFour

set_option Elab.async false

/-!
# The reciprocal sum of the prime factors when neither `3` nor `5` divides `N`

For an odd perfect number `N` with `3 ∤ N` and `5 ∤ N` we prove
`0.66747288 < ∑_{p ∣ N} 1/p` (`sum_inv_gt_66747288_of_not_three_not_five`). This replaces the
constant `0.667363` of `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFour` in this case.

Comparison with the literature. The rows are the four classes of `gcd(N, 15)`; each entry is a
lower bound and an upper bound for `∑_{p ∣ N} 1/p`, read from the source.

* `1970`: the bounds of Suryanarayana and Hagis (1970), as reprinted in Table 1 (page 370) of
  G. L. Cohen, "On odd perfect numbers (II), multiperfect numbers and quasiperfect numbers",
  J. Austral. Math. Soc. Ser. A 29 (1980), 369 to 384.
* `1980`: Table 2 (page 370) of the same paper, Cohen's improved bounds.
* `2024`: Proposition 1 (page 3) of J. Zelinsky, "The sum of the reciprocals of the prime
  divisors of an odd perfect or odd primitive non-deficient number", arXiv:2402.14234 (version
  5, 2025), which summarizes the results of Cohen, Hagis and Suryanarayana; its values are those
  of the column `1970`.
* `proved`: `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFour`, `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumUpper` and this file.

| gcd(N, 15) | 1970               | 1980               | 2024               | proved              |
|------------|--------------------|--------------------|--------------------|---------------------|
| 15         | 0.596063, 0.673770 | 0.596063, 0.673634 | 0.596063, 0.673770 | 0.5960631, 0.673634 |
| 3          | 0.603831, 0.657304 | 0.604707, 0.657304 | 0.603831, 0.657304 | 0.604706, 0.657304  |
| 5          | 0.647387, 0.678036 | 0.647649, 0.677637 | 0.647387, 0.678036 | 0.647648, 0.677637  |
| 1          | 0.667450, 0.693148 | 0.667472, 0.693148 | 0.667450, 0.693148 | 0.66747288, log 2   |

In the class `gcd(N, 15) = 1` of this file the constant `0.66747288` is ahead of the lower bound
`0.667450` of Proposition 1 of Zelinsky's preprint. Its new bounds (Propositions 4, 5, 6 and 15,
Corollaries 16 and 17) are stated in terms of the smallest prime factor `p₁` and the surplus
`S(n)`, not by the four classes; its upper bound `log 2 - 11/(50 p₁²)` (Corollary 16, page 13)
assumes `p₁ ≥ 11`. Against Cohen (1980) the constant is ahead as well, in the seventh decimal
place.

Cohen's lower bound in the last case (Theorem 1 (ii)) is `S₂(7, ..., 59, 307; 311)`, about
`0.66747275`. He shows (page 379) that without new restrictions on the prime factors or their
exponents this bound can be improved in the seventh decimal place at best, up to the limit
`S₂(7, ..., 59, 307; ∞)`, about `0.66747289`. The bound here uses no new restriction and stays
below that limit: it gains in the seventh decimal place only, by deciding every prime below
`5000` instead of estimating all prime factors above `307` at once.

The method. Every prime factor of `N` is at least `7`, and `log 2 < ∑_{p ∣ N} log(p/(p-1))`
(`log_two_lt_sum_log`). The abundancy is not used. Computations outside Lean put the infimum of
the relaxed problem (a set of primes `p ≥ 7` with `log 2 < ∑ log(p/(p-1))`; minimize `∑ 1/p`)
in the interval from `0.6674728857` to `0.6674728915`, with or without the condition that the
least component abundancies have product at most `2`; an explicit set of primes near the upper
end is `7, ..., 59`, `307` and a tail of large primes. The check
`fiveCheck` walks through the odd primes `7 ≤ p < 5000` in increasing order and decides for each
whether it divides `N`. Let `R` be the prime factors not decided yet, all at least the next entry
`m` of the list (or `m = 5000` at the end), and `r`, `u` the scaled sums of `1/p` (rounded down)
and of the upper bounds `u(p)/10^15 ≥ log(p/(p-1))` (from `fiveTable`) over the decided prime
factors. A branch stops as soon as `t < r` or, by `log(p/(p-1)) ≤ (2m - 3)/(2(m - 2)) * (1/p)`
for `p ≥ m`, `t ≤ r + (2m - 4)/(2m - 3) * (10^15 log 2 - u)`. The cover of the primes is checked
by `fiveGap`: between consecutive entries every number is even or has a small divisor. The table
was computed outside Lean (generator `scripts/ReciprocalSum/gen_recip5.py`); the kernel checks
again every table entry, every gap and every branch.
-/

open Finset

namespace OddPerfect

